# Vercel Speed Insights Installation

Successfully installed and configured Vercel Speed Insights for this Next.js project.

## Changes Made

### 1. Installed Package
- Added `@vercel/speed-insights` version ^2.0.0 to dependencies
- Updated package-lock.json with new dependency tree

### 2. Updated Root Layout (src/app/layout.tsx)
- Imported `SpeedInsights` component from `@vercel/speed-insights/next`
- Added `<SpeedInsights />` component to the root layout's body section
- Placed after `<AppProviders>` to ensure proper initialization order

## Implementation Details

Following the official Vercel Speed Insights documentation (https://vercel.com/docs/speed-insights/quickstart), the implementation uses the Next.js App Router pattern:

```typescript
import { SpeedInsights } from '@vercel/speed-insights/next'

// Added to body in RootLayout component
<SpeedInsights />
```

This approach:
- Works with Next.js App Router (v13.5+)
- Automatically tracks page performance metrics
- Sends data to Vercel's Speed Insights dashboard
- Has zero impact on bundle size for visitors (loaded asynchronously)

## Files Modified

- `package.json` - Added @vercel/speed-insights dependency
- `package-lock.json` - Updated with new dependency lock
- `src/app/layout.tsx` - Added SpeedInsights component

## Next Steps

Once deployed to Vercel:
1. Speed Insights will automatically start collecting performance data
2. Access the dashboard via your Vercel project's "Speed Insights" tab
3. Data will appear after real users visit your site (typically within a few days)

## Notes

- The implementation preserves all existing code structure
- No breaking changes were introduced
- The component is placed at the end of the body tag as recommended
- Pre-existing build errors related to missing Supabase environment variables are unrelated to this change
